    words_note='Five words for today. A label is the correct answer attached to an example, such as spam or a house price. Classification means predicting a category, and predicting a number is called regression. A decision tree is a model made of yes-or-no questions. k-nearest neighbours, written k-NN, predicts from the k most similar examples, found with a distance metric. And cross-validation splits the data into k folds, trains on all but one, tests on the held-out fold, and rotates. The last one matters most when you have little data. We start with the first two, label and classification, on a spam filter.',
    cover_note='Welcome back. Last time a picture became numbers, a neuron scored them, and a small network learned its weights from labelled digits. Today we ask a wider question: how does a machine learn a rule from examples, and how much can you trust what it learned? We start with the plainest form of machine learning. You will see the four types of learning, a decision tree, and k-nearest neighbours. As before, each idea starts with an everyday example, a spam filter, an umbrella, a house price, and then the numbers and the standard name. After that comes the business application, on real data: the forty Nile Valley Foods customer messages and a table of expense claims. The set is small, so we also learn to ask how honest a score is.',
