              'The distance slide uses an invented example on the same kind of grid: new house N = 80 m\u00b2, 3 km (4 squares across from the 40 m\u00b2 edge, 3 up); P = 110 m\u00b2, 7 km; Q = 90 m\u00b2, 4 km; R = 60 m\u00b2, 4 km; Euclidean distances 5, 1.4, 2.2. The k-nearest neighbours house slide: A 250, C 270, B 190 are the three nearest, mean 236.7 (UGX 237 million); F 300, E 120, D 330 are farther. The worksheet uses Manhattan distance (across + up); say it is a quicker count that usually names the same nearest house, and that Session 4 names both measures with dot product and cosine similarity. The umbrella tree and its 12 days are invented (clear 5 days, 0 rained; cloudy with forecast over 50%: 5 days, 4 rained; cloudy with forecast 50% or less: 2 days, 0 rained). Gini impurity: all 12 days 1 - (1/3)\u00b2 - (2/3)\u00b2 = 0.444; cloudy 1 - (4/7)\u00b2 - (3/7)\u00b2 = 0.490; clear 0; weighted (7 x 0.490 + 0) / 12 = 0.286. The word-count slide uses invented messages and four chosen words. The recorded word-based scores in this session used TF-IDF weights with cosine similarity: the nearest-neighbour slides name them only as a pointer to Session 4, with a one-line meaning.',
